.TH CLAUDE-WAKER 1 "2026-08-06" "claude-waker 0.2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
claude\-waker \- keep Claude Code sessions in iTerm2 awake on a schedule
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B claude\-waker
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B claude\-waker
periodically types a message into iTerm2 sessions whose name matches a pattern.
It exists so that Claude Code sessions which have stopped and are waiting for
input can be told to
.B continue
while you are away.
.PP
Run with no arguments on a terminal it lists the sessions currently open in
iTerm2, asks which to wake, and then presents an interactive menu. There is no
default target: it never guesses which session you meant. Given any flag or
subcommand it runs non\-interactively, so it is equally at home in a script or a
cron entry. The subcommand may appear before or after the flags.
.PP
The chosen target, message, count and interval are remembered, so a later bare
invocation resumes where you left off. See
.B REMEMBERING
below.
.PP
It talks to iTerm2 through
.BR osascript (1)
and has no other dependencies. It will not launch iTerm2; if iTerm2 is not
running it exits 3.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Run the wake loop.
.TP
.B menu
Force the interactive menu.
.TP
.B list
List the current iTerm2 sessions with their tty and state.
.TP
.B status
Show the resolved settings and which sessions currently match, then exit
without sending anything.
.TP
.B install\-plugin
Install a "Claude Waker" entry into iTerm2's Scripts menu.
.TP
.B config
Show the config and state file paths and the effective settings.
.TP
.B forget
Clear the remembered choices.
.TP
.BR version ", " help
Print the version or the usage summary.
.SH TARGETING
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-match " " \fIPATTERN\fR
Match sessions by name. Repeatable; a session is targeted when any pattern
matches. There is no default \- with no target at all,
.B claude\-waker
asks you to pick from the sessions open in iTerm2.
.IP
A pick is stored as a derived substring rather than the exact title or the
session id, because Claude Code rewrites the title while it works and ids die
with the tab. Leading status glyphs and the trailing job name are stripped, so
.RB \(lq "\(ct dev3" \(rq
becomes
.BR dev3 .
The derived pattern is shown and can be edited before it is used; typing numbers
there picks again rather than setting a pattern.
.IP
The session
.B claude\-waker
is running in is left out of the picker and is never matched by a pattern or by
.BR \-\-all :
writing into it types the message at the waker instead of at Claude. Naming it
with
.B \-\-session\-id
or
.B \-\-tty
still targets it.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-match\-mode " " \fIMODE\fR
One of
.BR contains " (default), " exact ", " glob ", " regex .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
Match case\-insensitively.
.TP
.BR \-\-session\-id " " \fIUUID\fR
Target one exact session. Repeatable. Survives renames.
.TP
.BR \-\-tty " " \fIDEV\fR
Target by tty. Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Target every session.
.TP
.BR \-\-exclude " " \fIPATTERN\fR
Skip sessions whose name contains \fIPATTERN\fR. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-first
Only the first match.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-match
Exit non\-zero instead of warning when nothing matches.
.TP
.BR \-\-max\-targets " " \fIN\fR
Refuse to run if more than \fIN\fR sessions match.
.SH MESSAGE
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-text " " \fISTR\fR
The text to type. Default
.BR continue .
Repeat the flag to rotate between several messages across iterations.
.TP
.BR \-\-text\-file " " \fIFILE\fR
Read the message from a file.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-newline
Type the text without pressing Enter.
.TP
.B \-\-esc\-first
Send Escape before the text, clearing any half\-typed line.
.PP
The message is passed to AppleScript as an argument rather than interpolated
into the script source, so quotes, backslashes and
.B $(...)
arrive verbatim and are never evaluated.
.SH SCHEDULE
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-count " " \fIN\fR
How many sends. Default 16.
.B 0
means no limit.
.TP
.B \-\-forever
Same as
.BR "\-\-count 0" .
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-every " " \fIDUR\fR
Gap between sends. Default
.BR 45m .
.TP
.BR \-\-jitter " " \fIDUR\fR|\fIN\fB%\fR
Randomise each gap by plus or minus this much.
.TP
.BR \-\-delay " " \fIDUR\fR
Wait before the first send.
.TP
.BR \-\-at " " \fIHH:MM\fR
Make the first send at a wall\-clock time.
.TP
.BR \-\-until " " \fIHH:MM\fR
Stop sending after this time.
.TP
.BR \-\-deadline " " \fIDUR\fR
Stop after this much elapsed time.
.TP
.B \-\-legacy\-timing
Reproduce the asymmetric sleep of the original shell loop, which slept only for
the first half of its iterations.
.PP
Durations are plain seconds
.RB ( 2700 )
or unit strings:
.BR 90s ", " 45m ", " 1h30m ", " 2d .
There are \fIN\-1\fR waits for \fIN\fR sends; nothing sleeps after the last one.
.SH GUARDS
.TP
.B \-\-if\-idle
Skip sessions that are streaming output at that moment. This maps onto iTerm2's
.B is processing
property, which is true while Claude is producing an answer and false while it
waits for input. Note that a session blocked on a silent command, such as
.BR sleep ,
also reads as idle because nothing is being written to the terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-if\-prompt
Only send when the session is at a shell prompt. Requires iTerm2 shell
integration; without it no session appears to be at a prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-stop\-if\-gone
Exit when a targeted session disappears.
.SH OUTPUT
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Errors only.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Extra detail, including session ids in
.BR list .
.TP
.BR \-\-log " " \fIFILE\fR
Append a timestamped record of every send.
.TP
.B \-\-notify
Post a macOS notification on each wake.
.TP
.BR \-\-countdown ", " \-\-no\-countdown
Force the live countdown on or off. It is on by default on a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-cmd
Print the equivalent command line and exit.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Never prompt; skip the menu and the session picker.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-remember
Do not save these choices for next time.
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
While the countdown is running:
.BR s
send now,
.BR p
pause or resume,
.BR +
and
.BR \-
shift the next wake by five minutes,
.BR q
quit. Ctrl\-C exits cleanly and reports how many wakes were delivered.
.SH REMEMBERING
The target, message, count, interval and guards of the last run are written to
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/claude\-waker/state
(or
.I ~/.local/state/claude\-waker/state
) and used as the defaults next time, so a bare
.B claude\-waker
resumes the previous setup.
.PP
A configuration file takes precedence over the remembered state, and flags take
precedence over both, so a hand\-written config is never overwritten by whatever
was last run. Command\-line values replace the remembered ones rather than
adding to them:
.B "\-m dev3"
after a run against
.B dev4
targets only
.BR dev3 .
.PP
.B "claude\-waker forget"
clears the file;
.B \-\-no\-remember
runs without writing it.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/claude\-waker/config
or
.IR ~/.config/claude\-waker/config .
Simple
.I key = value
lines, with optional
.BI [profile: name ]
sections selected by
.BR \-p .
Profiles layer on top of the top\-level keys.
.PP
Precedence is flags, then profile, then top\-level config, then built\-in
defaults. The file is parsed against a whitelist of known keys; it is never
sourced or evaluated, so it cannot execute code.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
iTerm2 is not running.
.TP
.B 4
Nothing matched, or a guard stopped the run.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Pick a session, then repeat that setup from then on:
.B claude\-waker
.TP
Repeat whatever was last run:
.B claude\-waker run
.TP
Every session that is not mid\-answer, indefinitely:
.B claude\-waker run \-a \-\-if\-idle \-\-forever \-e 30m
.TP
Target either of two sessions:
.B claude\-waker run \-m dev3 \-m dev4
.TP
See what would be targeted without sending:
.B claude\-waker status \-m dev3
.TP
See what is out there:
.B claude\-waker list
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/claude-waker/config
Configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/claude-waker/state
Remembered choices from the last run.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/iTerm2/Scripts/Claude Waker.py
Installed by
.BR "claude\-waker install\-plugin" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR osascript (1)
.PP
https://github.com/vali\-m/iterm2\-claude\-waker
.SH AUTHOR
Valentin Marginean
